                alt: "Feel free to tear it apart. It's not really my opinion; I just picked a side.",
                altChunk: "Feel free to tear it apart",
                original: "So, I thought this wasn't normal, and this isn't. \"So, for for some people, that can actually help their mental health. When everybody is the same, people tend to compete with each other. Knowing that other ways of living exist can make you're you you more open-minded and relaxed, and at the end of the day, happier. In the end, happier. Finally, education: this is closely related to happiness. Foreign workers are young and they're they'll have kids. When children from different backgrounds study alongside Japanese kids, it benefits both sides. From a young age, they learn that being different isn't bad, and that there isn't just one moral role model like we had growing up. It's not just schools. As adults, Japanese workers will work alongside foreign workers. There might be language There might be language barriers and cultural differences, but overcoming them helps us grow. We become kinder, more compassionate, and more responsible citizens. For all these reasons, I think welcoming foreign workers is a great opportunity, and it's the right thing to do.\" So, what do you think? Uh, you know, you don't you don't have to, you know, casually, you don't have to comment on my opinions. It's not my opinions, you know, I I took a side, otherwise. But what do you think casually, like, uh, yeah, but, uh, you know, you can say in your country's situation, not, you know, because you are not Japanese.",
