                alt: "",
                original: "[AI が英訳したスピーチ — 朗読] I believe the government should place a certain limit on how much wealth a person can accumulate for three reasons. First, without a clear limit, the rich will keep getting richer, while the poor get poorer. This trend cannot stop on its own. The only institution with enough power to stop it is the nation-state, which is still the largest unit of governance we have. So, the government has to take a top-down approach. Second, when wealth is concentrated in a few individuals, so is influence. In today's world, that influence spreads instantly through social media and other platforms. This means the impact of extremely wealthy people is on a completely different scale, and has a different meaning compared to the past. Finally, there is a question of how we define happiness. In our current society, the more money you have, the more respected you are, and the more likely you are to be seen as happy. You can buy a big house, throw parties, and show off, but humans naturally compare themselves to others. Even if someone is already well off, they may feel jealous because the neighbor earns many times more. These negative feelings come from the assumption that there's no limit to how much wealth a person can gain. If the government set the limit, it could ease some of this envy and resentment. So, for these three reasons, I believe the government should put a limit on personal wealth. Thank you for listening. What do you think?",
