                fix: "Because you don't have to, right? I'd never turned my phone off, not once. So the AI started up: \"How can I help you?\" Dude, you can help me by turning off! I didn't know how to turn my phone off. I tried again, and it popped up again. Oh my god. That AI was the only one in the whole building who was trying to help me that day. I was swiping like crazy, and then the proctor just walked over, and my phone got confiscated, because I couldn't turn it off. My reputation was ruined before I'd answered a single question. Now, there was this one woman in the very front row, right in front of the speaker — a machine, like a radio. She was so well prepared it was almost scary. Two pencils lined up perfectly, an eraser, a watch, and a spare watch. She knew everything. She knew the room didn't have a clock. I don't know why, but nowadays there's no big clock on the wall. And she knew the audio echoes if you sit far away. She was a pro, a veteran. Right before the test, a guy raised his hand and said, \"Sorry, I forgot my watch.\" The proctor said, \"There's no clock in this room.\" The guy just froze. And then this very nice woman, without saying a word, handed him her spare watch. I was a little moved. He bowed, and she gave a little nod. It was honestly the most touching moment of the day. And I was sitting in the back thinking, give me one too. Because — the article doesn't say this — I didn't have an analog watch. So on Saturday, I bought one at — do you know Daiso? It's like a 100-yen shop. A dollar store.",
